\description{
Estimate conditional plug-in interaction shifts \eqn{b_{jc}} within
\verb{[-max_abs, max_abs]} holding a fitted \code{RSM}, \code{PCM}, or one-family \code{GPCM}
baseline calibration and Person measures fixed (FACETS Table 13 screen; for
jointly estimated interactions use \code{facet_interactions} in \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} and
\code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}}).

\code{interaction_effect_table()} returns the fixed-effect interaction block
estimated by \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} when \code{facet_interactions} is supplied. These are
model-estimated deviations from the additive main-effects MFRM, not the
residual screening statistics returned by \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}}.
}
\details{
For additive predictor \eqn{\eta_{njc} = \theta_n - \delta_j - \beta_c +
b_{jc}}, interior solutions solve \eqn{\sum_n w_{njc}\{X_{njc} -
E_{njc}(b_{jc})\} = 0} with conditional SE
\eqn{[\sum_n w_{njc}\mathrm{Var}_{njc}(\hat b)]^{-1/2}} (including slope
factors and conditional LR/profile columns in one-family GPCM). Screening \code{t}
tails use continuous Student \code{df = weighted_count - 1} (\code{ProbabilityStatus},
\code{ScreenStatus}); calibration uncertainty is not propagated.

mfrmr supports two-way interactions between non-person facets,
for example \code{facet_interactions = "Rater:Criterion"}. Each interaction matrix
is identified by zero marginal sums across both participating facets, so the
interaction estimates are separable from the two main effects. Positive values
indicate higher-than-expected scores for the facet-level combination under the
additive model; negative values indicate lower-than-expected scores.

Use this table for confirmatory model review after specifying the facet pair
of substantive interest. For exploratory screening without adding parameters
to the fitted model, use \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}} or \code{\link[=estimate_all_bias]{estimate_all_bias()}}.

With \code{comparison_review}, first fit the declared model using the review's
\code{fit_arguments} and the original ratings. The fit must match the retained
responses, origins, score coding, model, constraints, facet orientation,
step owner and population design. Only native, unadjusted RSM/PCM fits
without shrinkage are supported. The contrast is the declared weighted
sum of Group1-minus-Group2 interaction effects, in logits. Positive means
a relative shift toward higher coded scores; it is not an absolute school
effect or evidence of unfairness. Location and step effects are not added.

This route reevaluates the retained likelihood and full gradient without
optimization. Objective agreement must be within \code{1e-6} and the gradient
sup norm within \code{max(1e-4, 10 * reltol)}, matching the ordinary numerical
stopping tolerance. For MML it also evaluates \code{2 * q + 1} quadrature nodes,
requiring the finer-grid gradient to meet that tolerance and the absolute
negative-log-likelihood change per Person to be at most \code{1e-6}.
These are numerical checks, not statistical precision criteria. A failed
check retains the row with \code{Estimate = NA} and an explanation; the separate
\code{OptimizerEstimate} is a diagnostic trace. No refit or partial fit is run.

\code{EstimateStatus = "descriptive_only"} requires full conditional-map rank,
numerical convergence, current readiness information and no recorded
blocking condition or boundary exclusion. It does not certify finite-MLE
existence, global marginal identification or sampling performance. MML
retains \code{State = "not_evaluated"} for positive conditional ranks even when
a descriptive fitted contrast is available. \code{BoundaryState},
\code{FitReadiness}, \code{NumericalState} and \code{QuadratureState} remain separate.
\code{SE}, \code{Lower}, \code{Upper} remain missing and \code{FormalInferenceEligible} remains
false. A target-only identified contrast in a rejected full model remains
unavailable through this route. Save the returned table with \code{saveRDS()}
to preserve its review and checks without recalculation; CSV retains the
visible columns but not the attributes.

For a \code{mfrm_comparison_fit} created with \code{fit_scope = "comparisons"}, omit
\code{comparison_review}: the table already contains the declared targets and
their partial-fit checks. Extraction does not fit again. See \link{fit_mfrm_technical_details}
for its likelihood-preserving parameterization, availability requirements
and limits. For full RSM/PCM MML fits, \code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}} with the same
\code{comparison_review} separately supplies model-information intervals and
supported population-SD standardization; extraction here stays descriptive.
CML comparison fits add conditional-information SEs and pointwise
95 percent Wald limits for identified targets, with \code{conditional_wald} status;
unidentified rows remain missing. The ordinary full-fit route above remains unchanged.

\code{summary()} collects cell availability, magnitudes and conditional
screening results from a saved bias analysis.

For \code{summary()}: Read \code{overview}, the conditional all-zero \code{chi_sq}
screen and \code{final_iteration}; \code{top_rows} ranks absolute t and retains GPCM
profile columns when present. These reuse the screening qualifications above,
not a formal bias test. Use \link{mfrm_report_bias_interaction} for detailed review.

\code{plot()} method: Plot saved bias-interaction results

Inspect the pattern of conditional interaction estimates from \code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}}.
Start with ranked interactions, then use other views to understand the pattern.

For \code{plot()}:
Visualization front-end for \link{mfrm_report_bias_interaction} with multiple views.
With \code{draw = FALSE}, \code{plot_data(p)} includes \code{plot_long},
\code{plot_annotations}, \code{flag_summary}, and \code{plot_settings} in addition to the
view-specific \code{ranked_table}, \code{scatter_data}, \code{facet_profile}, and heatmap
components. Use these fields when rebuilding the same screening view in
plotly, Quarto, or a dashboard.
Saved report thresholds determine flags, reference lines and returned notes;
recreate \link{mfrm_report_bias_interaction} from the saved bias object to change
them. Scatter views attach aligned marginal histograms when
\code{marginal = "auto"} (with \eqn{n \ge 8}) or \code{"histogram"}. Conditional bounds and their status
remain separate from calibration-inclusive uncertainty.

This helper summarizes how many observations contribute to each
bias-cell estimate and flags sparse cells.

Branch behavior:
\itemize{
\item \code{"facets"}: keeps legacy manual-aligned column labels (\code{Sq},
\verb{Observd Count}, \verb{Obs-Exp Average}, \verb{Model S.E.}) for side-by-side
comparison with external workflows.
\item \code{"original"}: keeps compact field names (\code{Count}, \code{BiasSize}, \code{SE}) for
custom QC workflows and scripting.
}
}
